  "name": "Question 1",
  "problem": "Factor $9{n}^{6}+6{n}^{3}+1.$",
  "worked_solution": "We can introduce a new variable $z={n}^{3}$ and then rewrite the trinomial as a quadratic in terms of $n:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}9{n}^{6}+6{n}^{3}+1 & =9{({n}^{3})}^{2}+6{n}^{3}+1 \\\\  & =9{z}^{2}+6z+1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can factor the quadratic as the square of a sum, and then rewrite the final expression in terms of $n:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}9{z}^{2}+6z+1 & =(3z)^{2}+6z+1 \\\\  & ={(3z+1)}^{2} \\\\  & ={(3{n}^{3}+1)}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$",
